===Etymology 1===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*bʰer-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|offer}}, from {{inh|en|ang|offrian||offer or make a sacrifice}} rather than from {{der|en|fro|offre||offer}}, from {{m|fro|offrir||to offer}}, from {{der|en|la|offerō||to present, bring before}}. Compare {{cog|frr|offer||sacrifice, donation, fee}}, {{cog|nl|offer||offering, sacrifice}}, {{cog|de|Opfer||victim, sacrifice}}, {{cog|da|offer||victim, sacrifice}}, {{cog|is|offr||offering}}. See verb below.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# A [[proposal]] that has been made.
#: {{ux|en|What's in his '''offer'''?}}
#: {{ux|en|I decline your '''offer''' to contract.}}
#* {{RQ:Churchill Celebrity|chapter=4|passage=One morning I had been driven to the precarious refuge afforded by the steps of the inn, after rejecting '''offers''' from the Celebrity to join him in a variety of amusements. But even here I was not free from interruption, for he was seated on a horse-block below me, playing with a fox terrier.}}
# Something [[put forth]], [[bid]], [[proffer]]ed or [[tender]]ed.
#: {{ux|en|His '''offer''' was $3.50 per share.}}
# {{lb|en|legal}} An [[invitation]] to enter into a [[bind]]ing [[contract]] communicated to another party which contains [[term]]s sufficiently [[definite]] to create an enforceable contract if the other party accepts the invitation.
#: {{ux|en|His first letter was not a real '''offer''', but an attempt to determine interest.}}

