===Etymology 2===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*bʰer-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|offren}}, {{m|enm|offrien}}. In the religious senses inherited from {{der|en|ang|offrian||to offer, sacrifice, bring an oblation}}; otherwise from {{der|en|fro|ofrir}}. Both ultimately from {{der|en|la|offerō||to present, bestow, bring before|lit=to bring to}}, from {{der|en|la|ob}} + {{m|la|ferō||bring, carry}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*bʰer-||to carry, bear}}, later reinforced by {{cog|fro|offrir||to offer}}. Cognate with {{cog|ofs|offria||to offer}}, {{cog|odt|offrōn||to offer}}, {{cog|de|opfern||to offer}}, {{cog|non|offra||to offer}}. More at {{l|en|ob-}}, {{l|en|bear}}.

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[propose]] or express one's willingness (to do something).
#: {{ux|en|She '''offered''' to help with her homework.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To present in words; to proffer; to make a proposal of; to suggest.
#: {{ux|en|Everybody '''offered''' an opinion.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[place]] at someone’s [[disposal]]; to [[present]] (something) to be either accepted or turned down.
#: {{syn|en|extend}}
#: {{ux|en|He '''offered''' use of his car for the week.}}
#: {{ux|en|He '''offered''' his good will for the Councilman's vote.}}
#* {{RQ:Hough Purchase Price|chapter=II|text=Carried somehow, somewhither, for some reason, on these surging floods, were these travelers,{{nb...}}. Even such a boat as the ''Mount Vernon'' '''offered''' a total deck space so cramped as to leave secrecy or privacy well out of the question, even had the motley and democratic assemblage of passengers been disposed to accord either.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2013-06-28|author=w:Joris Luyendijk| volume=189| issue=3| page=21| magazine=w:The Guardian Weekly| title=Our banks are out of control|url=http://www.guardian.co.uk/commentisfree/joris-luyendijk-banking-blog/2013/jun/19/banking-britain-beyond-control| passage=Seeing the British establishment struggle with the financial sector is like watching an alcoholic{{nb...}}. Until 2008 there was denial over what finance had become.{{...}}But the scandals kept coming,{{nb...}}. A broad section of the political class now recognises the need for change but remains unable to see the necessity of a fundamental overhaul. Instead it '''offers''' fixes and patches.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[present]] (something) for [[sale]].
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To present (something) to God or gods, as a gesture of [[worship]] or as a [[sacrifice]].
#* {{RQ:KJV|Exodus|xxix|36|text=Thou shalt '''offer''' every day a bullock for a sin offering for atonement.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|of a thing}} To [[present]] (something) to the [[sight]] etc.; to [[provide]] for [[use]], [[consideration]] etc.
#: {{syn|en|offer up|showcase}}
#: {{ux|en|The city '''offers''' beautiful architecture.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|engineering}} To [[place]] (something) in a [[position]] where it can be [[add]]ed to an existing [[mechanical]] [[assembly]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2009|author=Roger Williams| title=Triumph TR2, 3, 3A, 4 & 4A| passage=The next stage is to remove and replace the top part of the right side lip, and '''offer''' the lid to the car to ensure all the shapes and gaps are okay.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To bid, as a price, reward, or wages.
#: {{ux|en|I '''offered''' twenty dollars for it. The company is '''offering''' a salary of £30,000 a year.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[happen]], to present itself.
#* {{RQ:Dryden Georgics|4|passage=The occasion '''offers''', and the youth complies.}}
#* {{RQ:Cleland Fanny Hill|1|72|The opportunity however did not '''offer''' till next morning, for ''Phœbe'' did not come to bed till long after I was gone to ſleep:}}
#* {{RQ:Melville Moby-Dick|chapter=2|page=7|passage=Much was I disappointed upon learning that the little packet for Nantucket had already sailed, and that no way of reaching that place would '''offer''', till the following Monday.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete|outside|Appalachia}} To [[attempt]] (to do something); to make an attempt (in older texts, typically used with ''at'').
#* {{RQ:Bacon War with Spain|passage=I will not '''offer''' at that I cannot master.}}
#* {{RQ:L'Estrange Fables of Aesop|passage=He would be '''offering''' at the shepherd's voice.}}
#* {{RQ:Swift Conduct of the Allies|pages=19–20|pageref=19|passage=[W]ithout '''offering''' at any other Remedy, without taking time to conſider the Conſequences, or to reflect on our own Condition, we haſtily engaged in a War which hath coſt us ſixty Millions; {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Fielding Tom Jones|passage=Here Jones, after expressing the utmost uneasiness, '''offered''' to stop her mouth:—“Hey-day! why sure, Mr Jones, you will let me speak; I speaks no scandal, for I only says what I heard from others {{...}}}}
#* '''1969''', Leonard Roberts, ''Old Greasybeard: Tales from the Cumberland Gap'', page 132:
#*: [This] giant didn't '''offer''' to hurt Tom until he found out how brave he was. 
#* '''1993''', Thomas Burton, ''Take Up Serpents'', reprinted in '''2025''' as ''Serpent Handling Believers''
#*: As soon as [serpents] were touched by those under the power they wilted and never '''offered''' to bite any one.
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To put in opposition to; to manifest in an offensive or defensive way; to threaten.
#: {{ux|en|to '''offer''' violence to somebody}}
#: {{ux|en|The peasants '''offered''' no resistance as they were rounded up.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=15 Dec 79|volume=7|number=21|journal=Gay Community News|page=3|title=Two Assaulted in Bay Village in Separate Incidents|text=A car turned the corner and stopped, and three or four people emerged and approached her, grabbing her purse. When she '''offered''' resistance, one of the assailants took out a knife and stabbed her in the lower abdomen and legs.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2013|author=Andrew Wiest|title=Vietnam: A View from the Front Lines|page=125|text=The Viet Cong and NVA tended to '''offer''' battle only when they felt that they held a tactical advantage – if they didn't they usually hunkered down in their nearly invisible bunkers and let the Americans pass.}}

